1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:06,026
Comparing Habitats A school display compared a tropical jungle an open prairie

2
00:00:06,224 --> 00:00:10,789
and a wooded valley One map marked the Tropic of Cancer while

3
00:00:10,803 --> 00:00:15,724
another showed where birds migrate The children noticed an abundance of life

4
00:00:15,737 --> 00:00:20,276
in every scene but the dominant forms differed In a short film

5
00:00:20,408 --> 00:00:23,776
a cuckoo called near a maple a snake described as a serpent

6
00:00:23,789 --> 00:00:28,382
moved through leaves and a predator pursued its prey The teacher warned

7
00:00:28,395 --> 00:00:33,790
against calling an unfamiliar creature an alien monster Its appearance might seem

8
00:00:33,803 --> 00:00:38,632
peculiar to us yet it belonged to its own environment We examined

9
00:00:38,645 --> 00:00:41,487
how a web could trap an insect and how a plant's growth

10
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,974
might be limited by scarcity of water No habitat contained perpetual plenty

11
00:00:47,882 --> 00:00:52,908
Ecological understanding grows when we compare relationships rather than rank landscapes by

12
00:00:52,921 --> 00:00:57,645
beauty Even a place that appears empty can sustain life adapted to

13
00:00:57,658 --> 00:01:00,158
conditions that would challenge other organisms
